            "lens": "review-risk",
            "findings": [],
            "evidence": [
              "engine/settings/quote.go: shellQuote returns a path made only of letters, digits and _@%+=:,./- unchanged. Anything else is wrapped in single quotes with each embedded single quote written as quote, backslash, quote, quote. That is the standard POSIX-safe form. A binary path holding $, backticks, spaces or newlines cannot break out of the command, so the change closes an injection and breakage vector rather than opening one.",
              "All nine hook builders (approve guard, projection, minimalism and design in both events, sync trigger, review receipt, shaper guard) now quote the binary in both the command -v check and the call. The shaper guard fallback text still holds fixed guardmarkers constants, with no user input spliced in.",
              "engine/settings/merge.go entryContainsBinary also accepts the quoted path, so entries written before this change are still recognised and replaced, not duplicated. Uninstall ownership stays by substring, as it was at the base.",
              "Making the five legacy families repair means a foreign entry that holds our path inside a longer binary name and carries a family token is now replaced by merge. It was kept before. This is documented as a known owner-visible consequence, pinned by a test, and uninstall already removed such entries. The loss is limited to hooks that look like ours and the original stays in the .bak file, so I rate it at most a WARNING and report no finding.",
              "The G12 changes in bin/lib/apply.sh and bin/lib/longterm_mem.sh are comment and warning text only. The shell test covers the second-run behaviour. No security or data-loss impact.",
              "Five golden merge cases are skipped by name with recorded reasons. This reduces regression coverage but is not a reachable security harm."
            ]
          }
        }
